biostatistician, specifically in an academic health centre (Golbeck, 2017; Mazumdar et al., 2015; Spratt et al., 2017). We were not aware of any
papers focused on career development, mentoring and networking strategies or peer recognition. At that time, the executive committee of the
Section on Statistical Consulting (Section) of the American Statistical Association (ASA) received anecdotal reports that collaborative statisticians,
particularly in academia, were finding it difficult to achieve promotion or meaningful recognition for their contributions, whose challenges were
represented in a single article (Craig et al., 2018).
To further investigate the advancement concerns of collaborative statisticians, the Section launched a survey of all members in 2020. The
qualitative responses received from members confirmed that they faced limited opportunities for advancement, particularly when they were not
active in methodological development, with many expressing feelings of being undervalued and underrecognized. Members reported feeling
trapped in their careers without a clear pathway for progression because of their lack of first-author publications, opportunities to be the PI on a
grant or non-participation in grants focused on methodological development. These limitations for advancement existed in both statistics depart
ments and other academic divisions across the campus, that is, biostatistics cores and data centres. Additionally, a common theme was that
department chairs, deans and supervisors did not understand or appreciate the contribution made by collaborative statisticians. While these
challenges existed before 2021, the career effects were undoubtedly exacerbated by the COVID-19 pandemic marked by social isolation, remote
work and virtual meetings. These added conditions had been going on for nearly a year when the P2P Committee was formed.
The P2P Committee originated as a team of 12 Section volunteers tasked with creating tools and materials to support the professional
development of collaborative statisticians in academia. This initiative aimed particularly at those who felt isolated in their roles. Five of the seven
authors of this manuscript were members of the original P2P Committee. Each member had confronted or was facing barriers to promotion in
their respective organization despite their best efforts at self-advocacy. While the members' reporting structures were not identical, each felt
isolated in their organization, without a clearly written policy or pathway for advancement. In many cases, members' job descriptions were
narrowly focused on assisting collaborative partners and advancing the research mission of the organizations, yet members were evaluated based
on outcomes expected of those doing independent theoretical or methodological research. This common cause allowed the P2P Committee to
bond quickly, identify common career objectives and provide an incentive to be engaged in our mission: to develop recommendations and tools
to help the advancement of non-tenure-track collaborative/consulting academic statisticians (Table 1). As the P2P Committee gained momentum,
other colleagues in similar situations joined and the Committee quickly expanded to nearly 20 members.
In this paper, we discuss the development of the Committee, its impact on the community of collaborative statisticians, what Committee
members have learned and future areas for scholarship and professional development.
2 | CONNECTING THE COMMUNITY
The P2P Committee found that its mission and goals resonated with many collaborative statisticians. This shared interest facilitated connections,
fostering the formation of a community that has been steadily growing. One of the Committee's initial decisions to make all produced materials
freely available to the public, without restrictions based on education, membership or career path enabled the community to be more inclusive.
The Committee recognized that many collaborative statisticians faced similar obstacles to career advancement and would benefit from the
dissemination of its work. The ASA supported this decision which allowed the P2P Committee to connect with the larger community of isolated
statisticians and include non-members.
The P2P Committee decided one of the most effective ways to meet our charge was to use free webinars as a way to communicate with a
broader community. These real-time information sessions provided a venue for a variety of speakers to present, which allowed us to expand
beyond the expertise and experience of our small committee. This format also allowed for the audience's active participation which encouraged
attendees' involvement. The webinars are recorded and made available on the ASA Section's YouTube channel (@amstatconsultingsection7036).
In the second year, we made a concerted effort to increase our web and social media presence, providing summaries and links to each of
our webinars via our ASA Section microsite: https://community.amstat.org/cnsl/networking/networking-acadpromotion and LinkedIn group
(asa-section-on-statistical-consulting). We created an X account (@ASA_CNSL_P2P) so anyone could follow us to be notified about our upcoming
offerings and receive live updates during webinars. Lastly, we created a logo for the P2P Committee and developed our version of the P2P brand
(Figure 1).
