breakout groups allowing attendees to directly ask questions. The members of the P2P Committee have rotated roles, serving as moderators,
speakers or in supportive roles such as data analytics, advertising, chat monitor or webhost. The Committee consciously chooses speakers to
represent different career paths, institution types and demographic profiles to foster diversity and inclusivity. Participants who register for the
webinars are surveyed about their interest in the topic and to submit any questions they may have. Results from the pre-survey in combination
with our ideas inform the speakers of the specific interests of the audience. This approach allows speakers to tailor their presentations and
address the most pressing and important questions. After the webinar, another survey is sent to garner feedback on the webinar and to help
identify targets for future work, keeping the topics relevant and engaging.
Typically, the invited speakers give a brief presentation, and these are followed by an open-panel discussion, allowing questions and
comments to be introduced through the virtual chat. Recent webinars have been extended to include breakout rooms to allow for networking
with the speakers and mimic the post-meeting informal discussions that often occur in an in-person setting. These smaller breakout rooms are
unrecorded and allow participants to engage in a more casual and social environment. In addition to the webinars, we have organized in-person
roundtables, oral presentations and discussions at multiple ASA meetings to further grow the community.
3 | COMMITTEE ACTIVITIES AND SCHOLARSHIP
The P2P Committee produced seven semi-annual webinars between May 2021 and June 2024 (American Statistical Association, Consulting
Section, n.d.). See Table 2 for titles of each of the webinars. Topics included career advice for staff statisticians, negotiation skills, addressing
burnout, developing a career as a collaborative statistician in the context of team science, peer review and preparing a faculty promotion packet.
Three of the webinars have been written up in AMSTAT news (Bolch et al., 2022; Leary, Sheng, et al., 2023; Mehta, Stedman, et al., 2022), and to
our knowledge, the webinar on burnout was the first public discussion of this topic specific to statisticians.
In addition to webinars, the P2P Committee members have given four oral presentations (Leary et al., 2022; Leary, Vasilopoulos, et al., 2023;
Stedman et al., 2023; Vasilopoulos, 2023) at professional conferences. These presentations focused on topics such as mentoring and building con
nections with isolated statisticians. The committee organized three roundtables (Bolch et al., 2021; Sheng et al., 2022; Vasilopoulos et al., 2022)
for the Joint Statistical Meetings, covering career development for staff and negotiation skills, and one workshop at the Conference on Statistical
Practice (Sheng et al., 2024) on developing promotion materials for collaborative statisticians (Mehta, Leary, et al., 2022). Committee members
published an article on reducing isolation among statisticians. As the community expanded, we also gained co-sponsorship of events by the
San Francisco Bay Area chapter of the ASA and the Caucus for Women in Statistics. These sponsors helped encourage more statisticians from
industry to attend, further enhancing the diversity of the community.
