4 | ATTENDANCE AND SATISFACTION
Attendance at our events, both at webinars and conferences, has exceeded expectations including a sellout of the first roundtable at JSM in
2021. The registrants for the webinars exceed the limited capacity of the Section's Zoom licence; a P2P Committee member whose institutional
licence could accommodate is now used. Attendance continues to show that topics selected are relevant as webinars average over 200 registrants
and 121 attendees (see Table 2). Attendee's satisfaction is high, with 73% of participants giving the webinar the highest possible rating. Further
more, our webinars are reaching an audience broader than initially intended, on average including just under 30% non-academic statisticians and
20% non-ASA members (see Table 2). While attendance and satisfaction has been high, obtaining consistent feedback post-webinar has proven
difficult. The most successful but also most laborious method incorporates the questionnaire directly into Zoom. This method achieved a 100%
response rate in the Art of Negotiation webinar.
4.1 | Participant survey
In preparation for this manuscript, we emailed a survey to 630 past attendees and received 47 responses. Over half of the responders (Table 3)
identified as female (n = 25, 53%) and around a quarter were non-white (n = 10, 24%). Approximately two thirds of respondents held a doctorate,
while the remainder had master's degrees. Notably, 81% worked with a group of statisticians and 70% were in academia. The percentages of staff,
tenured/tenure-track faculty and non-tenured faculty were similar, with only one student responding. Titles ranged from junior- to senior-level
positions, including Statistician, Director and Assistant, Associate and Full Professor.
Regarding the time in their current position/role, about 30% were employed in their role for 1 year or less, while nearly 14% were employed
for over 10 years. Information about events reached 57% through the email list and 22% through the ASA communities list. Almost everyone
(98%) found that P2P events met or exceeded their expectations, and 93% planned to attend future events. Surprisingly, 15.2% of respondents
were not engaged with the ASA (Table 3).
5 | DISCUSSION, SCHOLARSHIP AND FUTURE DIRECTIONS FOR THE COMMITTEE
The P2P Committee has discovered that there are many collaborative statisticians working in diverse roles and having varied job responsibilities
and promotion opportunities. The roles they take can range from entry-level to seasoned veteran to leadership with managerial responsibility.
Some are isolated while others collaborate with peers or interact with individuals in related fields. Further confounding this diversity, employers
give differing titles to similar positions, and all seem to require different promotion criteria and have different long-term goals for their statisti
cians. During COVID lockdowns, collaborative statisticians recognized more clearly their isolation and realized the importance of connecting with
mentors and peers due to reduced interpersonal contact. The work of the P2P Committee helps fill this need by using webinars and virtual
